we just upgraded one of our decstation 5000/240s to a decstation
5000/260 by swapping the processor subsystem for a new one with an
R4400 and associated external cache. since we had been running ultrix
4.3 on our 240s previously, we also did a test install of ultrix 4.4
on the machine with the new processor.

for the most part, we're pretty happy with the upgrade -- many
binaries run more than twice as fast as they had previously.

unfortunately, one of our key workloads (a large simulator consisting
primarily of compiled T code -- T is a Scheme-like dialect of lisp)
fails and crashes, somewhat non-deterministically, during a start-up
phase when large amounts of compiled code are being loaded into the
running T image and run for the first time. these failures come in a
variety of flavors, ranging from the simple "illegal instruction"
variety to sanity checks in the T system failing and causing
exceptions to be signaled.

i'm assuming that this lossage is probably due to the R3000 -> R4400
processor subsystem upgrade, but we haven't yet ruled out that the
ultrix 4.3 -> 4.4 upgrade isn't a contributing factor. (we can do so
by upgrading one of our 240s to ultrix 4.4 and see if it has the same
problems; we will probably try this experiment in the near future.)

i'd be interested in hearing about other people in net.land who have
had similar compatibility problems or might have any insight as to why
we're having them in our situation.

i'll try to keep an eye on comp.sys.dec, but e-mail responses
(tuna@cag.lcs.mit.edu) are best. if there is sufficient interest, i'll
happily summarize to the net.

thanks in advance,

kirk
