Since I am the someone, I thought I'd post an update.

I got a clarification of the circumstances where the RRD43
wasn't well behaved.  OSF/1 V1.3 doesn't like booting off
of it.  And that problem was fixed in V2.0.  I think it
had something to do with the negociation of Fast vs. Slow
with the host, adapter and RRD43.

That cleared up I went on a hunt for an RRD43 so I could
try it on an ULTRIX V4.3A system I had handy.  I had two
RRD43s in a StorageWorks 5 1/4" form factor brick and
put them in a shelf I had handy.  They were the only 
devices on the bus.  I was using a 2-3 meter cable to
a PMAZ on a DECsystem 5000/200.

The devices were recognized on boot.  The first I tried
was a simple dd to /dev/null and this worked fine.  Then
I went in search of a CD with a file system on it.  I couldn't
find an ULTRIX disc so I used an OSF disk and mounted it:

# mount -f force -r /dev/rz8c /mnt

A couple of ls(1) commands to the directory worked fine.

I don't know what sort of problem Mr. Carter was having,
but it doesn't seem to be consistent among a very small
selection of ULTRIX V4.3A systems.

re: Supported hardware and things that may not work.

In the SPD (legal) sense, Supported sometimes means that something
has been tested and we know it doesn't work and can't support
it.  Sometimes it means that we just haven't gotten around
to testing it yet or the testing is finished when the SPD has
to go the printer.  Sometimes there's nothing wrong with the
device and it will work without a glitch for the life of the
device, system and OS you're running (*).  But, that's doesn't
make it supported, only the SPD can do that.

Sometimes a particular version of the OS will never support
something because a new version is released that does support
it.  The RRD43 is listed as being on V4.4 but not V4.3A.  Since
V4.4 replaces V4.3A there's no reason to do the backward testing.

(*) I'm running a TZ85 and RF73 on ULTRIX V4.3 on a DECsystem
5500.  Neither are supported, but have worked since I add
them to the system.  The TZ85 gives me 600 MB/sec during
backups instead of the 200something I'd get from the TLZ04.
The 2 GB the RF73 makes a better user disk than the RF31
it replaced...

