In article <2poh7k$3sl@usenet.INS.CWRU.Edu>, cs650@cleveland.Freenet.Edu (Alberto L. Zuniga) writes:
>
>What is the easiest way to recover from this error, and how can I 
>prevent it from happening again?  We have had this problem in the 
>past and solved it by refreshing the disk with a backup/restore.
>
>Any advice would be appreciated.
>
>$ copy sys$login:login.com []/log
>%COPY-E-OPENOUT, error opening LD_TSB2:[TSBVI_COMMON]LOGIN.COM;83 as output
>-RMS-E-CRE, ACP file create failed
>-SYSTEM-W-HEADERFULL, file header is full
>%COPY-W-NOTCOPIED, LD_TSB3:[ZUNIGA_A]LOGIN.COM;83 not copied
>$
>

You need to backup/restore the disk to defragment the volume's index file. 
This will solve your problem for now.

The problem is that the index file, used to map the various bits and pieces of
files, has become fragmented, so that you now have too many fragments of the
index file.  The solution would be to note the current size of the index file
(DIR/SIZE disk:[000000]INDEXF.SYS), remember the size, backup the disk, re-init
the disk with /HEADERS=nnnnn (the previous size of the index file, plus "a
little more"), then restore the disk (with BACKUP/NOINIT, to preserve the size
of the index file you just created):

	$ DIR/SIZE disk:[000000]INDEXF.SYS	! note the size
	$ DISMOUNT/NOUNLOAD disk		! to mount the disk privately
	$ MOUNT disk label			! mount the disk privately
	$ BACKUP/IMAGE disk tape:saveset.name/save
	$ DISMOUNT/NOUNLOAD disk
	$ INITIALIZE/HEADERS=nnnn disk label	! 'nnnn' the size from above
	$ MOUNT disk label
	$ BACKUP/NOINIT/IMAGE tape:saveset.name/save disk
	$ DISMOUNT/NOUNLOAD disk
	$ MOUNT/SYSTEM disk label logical

