Hah!  You've found one of the hidden copyright notices.  Can you find the
other?  (I don't mean those in the kit's command files either.)


|>        As with DCL command verbs,  qualifiers  are  not parsed past the
|>    first  four characters (unless they are negated, in which case  it's
|>    six characters).  There may be some exceptions, but I don't know  of
|>    any  off the top of my head.  

There are a few exceptions to the four character uniqueness rule for
verbs - C is always CONTINUE, E is always EXAMINE.  These are special
cases in the CLD for DCL.  It use to be that keywords were also subject to
a four-character rule, but this was eliminated a number of years ago.  The
OpenVMS documentation warns that the 4-character rule might be eliminated
for verbs and qualifiers in the future so you are well advised to fully spell
them out in command procedures.  I don't know of any exceptions for
qualifiers.

I keep getting bit on one of our systems that "LO" is no longer unique for
LOGOUT now that they've installed LOTUS 1-2-3.
