> In article <1994Apr4.015026.6613@lugb.latrobe.edu.au>, cchd@lucifer.latrobe.edu.au (Huw Davies) writes:
>>In article <Cn2zJ0.9qH@knot.ccs.queensu.ca> ssclift@mach.me.queensu.ca (Simon Clift) writes:
>>>
>>>DEC once made a machine called a VAX 11/782.  It's a 2-CPU, shared
>>>memory version of the VAX 11/780.  These were never a great success
>>>commercially.  Nonetheless, of the 12 ever built, Ontario Hydro owns
>>>three of them, which are still in service running nuclear power station
>>>control room simulators.
>>
>>I find it hard to believe that DEC only ever produced 12 782s. [...]

> And I have seen several with 3 and 4 CPUs for fligth simulators.

Those weren't 782's per se, though they could be booted as such (using a max of
two CPUs, though).  Those were 780s with both per-CPU private memory and the
MA780 shared memory.  In the original use of these machines, VMS was booted
independently on each node, and applications could communicate through the
shared memory via shared memory global sections, mailboxes, and common event
flag clusters.  

A true 782 came with ONLY MA780 shared memory and could be booted only as a 
single VMS system with two CPUs in an asymmetric processing arrangement
(in essence, the second CPU handled only user-mode code).  

