In article <2o4g8a$ct@master.cs.rose-hulman.edu> I wrote:

> Greetings.
> 
> I have a DS5000/200 with two Fujitsu drives at rz0 and rz1.  I would like
> to be able to boot from either one at whim (5/rz0/vmunix or 5/rz1/vmunix).
> 
> My problem:  When I boot from 5/rz1, it works fine, until it remounts the  
> root partition.  It mounts /dev/rz0a instead of /dev/rz1a.

Thanks to the MANY who replied (about 2 dozen).

Ways to solve this problem are:

1. Don't change the SCSI ID of the disk after building it. :-)  If it's built
   at SCSI ID 1, it will boot fine at SCSI ID 1.  (This is what we actually
   did - rebuilt the disk (for the heck of it)).

2. Edit the kernel configuration file for the machine, which lives in
   /sys/conf/mips/MACHINE_NAME, and look for a line similar to:

	config          vmunix  root on rz0a  swap on rz0b  dumps on rz0b

   and change it to read:

	config          vmunix  root on rz1a  swap on rz1b  dumps on rz1b

3. One respondent suggested a limited generic kernel:

	config vmunix swap on boot

   It's limited because you can only have one swap space; the B
   partition of whatever the boot disk is.

Thanks again to the MANY who responded.  Sorry for the lateness of this  
summary... circumstances beyond my control, etc., etc.  :-)

