In article <1994Apr16.212521.277@elmrd6.ineab.ikea.se>, anos@elmrd6.ineab.ikea.se writes:
>Our plans are to stripe entire channels (make all devices on bus C one
>stripeset, all on E another stripeset) and then shadow device zero on
>channel C to device 0 on channel E. Same for channels D and F. This seems
>to give both maximum i/o distribution and security. Is there something I
>have forgot ?
>
>Q1. If YOU have any experience with striping/shadowing (bad or good :-),
>    please send your thoughts and/or tip to me.

I have Striping and I like it.  I am getting good throughput from my Stripe
sets.  I have 2 8 Gb, 2 4 Gb, and 1 3 Gb stripe sets hanging off of HSC's
>    
>Q2. What is (if any...) the most optimum configurations of the system
>    described above. I realize that it depends on weather I want maximum
>    security or speed, and how the i/o load looks like. But in general,
>    what *should* be the optimal mix ? 
>
I have seen the best performance when each member of a stripe set is on a 
different controller.  When more than 1 member of a stripe set was on the 
same HSC requestor, I could see in DECPS how the queue length went up and IO 
rate went down.  By putting each disk on a different requestor, my queue 
length went down, and my IO rate went up.  

These disks are used for large files.  They are storage areas for Hubble 
Space Telescoipe data that is being transferred from our old Data Archive 
system to our new one.  There is about 900Gb to transfer and process.  

That is how I do it for performance.  For data security, I would buy a UPS
to put the system on.  This should cut your downtime tremendously.  I have 
had this cluster up for over a year with no disk failures using RA90, RA72, 
and RA73 disk drives.  

One problem for large Stripe sets is backups and restores.  I have not run
into that yet.  But luckily for me, all of my stripe sets except for one
are scratch areas with no unrecreatable data.  

Good luck with your system.

