   The Alpha architecture specifies 64 registers: 32 floating point, and
   32 integer registers.  Each is 64 bits wide.  Registers R31 and F31 do
   have special uses.  And unlike the VAX architecture, the Alpha integer
   register layout is generally free of restrictions on register use.

   In other words, 64 registers *and* 16 bit displacements.

   Compilers will likely be improving instruction scheduling parallelism
   over time, and the number of instructions that can be issued in parallel
   on an individual Alpha CPU will also likely be increasing over time, the
   speed of the CPUs will likely be increasing, and the number of Alpha
   processors available in a system will likely be increasing over time.
   (The first two classes of improvements will likely remain relatively
   coupled, too.)

   And if superscaler instruction scheduling becomes difficult or infeasible
   for a particular application -- say due to disparate register usage, the
   number of I-units issuing superscaler, and the total number of integer
   registers available -- one can potentially take advantage of the same
   disparaties and issue the instructions in a fully parallel fashion in
   an SMP system.

