(S P Booth) writes:
>[...]
>This implies you ideally want to be interleaving the calculation of 6
>separate expressions.
>The architecture specifies 32 registers of which r31 always returns zero
>so this gives about 5 registers to each expression.
>This may be ok for many purposes but it is a bit cramped for mine.

I agree, and see two ways out:

  1. Decrease function unit latency inversely proportional to
     superscalar width.  That is, 3 cycle pipes for a 4-issue machine,
     and 2 cycle pipes for the ultimate 6-issue machine.

  2. Add hardware instruction scheduling and register renaming so that
     the compiler need not specify any more parallelism.

It's a crying shame that alpha doesn't have more registers.  Wasn't
there an ASPLOS paper by a DEC author showing the value of 64 regs?  I
attempted encoding an alpha-like instruction set with 6-bit instead of
5-bit register fields within 32 bits; it appeared to be feasible if
the address displacements and immediate fields were reduced from 16 to
14 bits in the two-register format.  That's not a bad compromise -- HP
gets impressive performance with 14-bit displacements.
--
Robert Heiss		studying EE at Cal Poly


