In Article <2p1hln$7jp@reeve.research.aa.wl.com>, leibowa@aa.wl.com (Allen
Leibowitz) wrote:
>a DECbridge 90.  After so many seconds of inactivity by a station
>on the "terminal" end of the bridge, it will flush the MAC address of
>the now silent station (no problem here)
>BUT
>it will not forward packets from the "backbone" side until it hears
>from the station on the far end again. 
>

The bridge does address and protocol type filtering using a hardware CAM.  
The CAM has insufficient storage for all of the MAC addresses in a large
LAN.  Instead, it learns only the addresses on one side of it.  
In order to accomodate a network re-configuration that would move an address
from one port to another, it must time-out addresses.

The requirement for IP networks is that the timeout value for the MAC address
database be greater than the timeout values for ARP tables of the IP hosts.
This will cause an ARP request and response to be transacted before the
MAC address data base entry times out.


Hugh


