In article <cboesel.21.0012DC85@crl.com>, cboesel@crl.com (Charles Boesel) writes:
>A friend of mine has a lot of business files on a DEC Rainbow 100 which he 
>wants to put onto MS-DOS disks. How can he do this?

Easy.  Run MS-DOS on the Rainbow!  It will write MS-DOS disks just fine.

Oh, you want to be able to read these disks on an IBM-PC or compatible?  Well,
that's different.  If the PC is an AT or better, and has a 1.2MB floppy disk
drive, then you want to get a device driver that will make the floppy drive act
like the Rainbow's RX50 floppy disk drive.  

On the DECPC forum on CompuServe (!GO DECPC), there are two such drivers:
RX50.ARC and RAINDO.* (can't remember if it's .ARC or .ZIP).  Just install 'em
on the PC, reboot and you'll be able to read the RX50 diskettes produced by the
Rainbow.

