Our networking people are experiencing some very bad behavior from
a DECbridge 90.  After so many seconds of inactivity by a station
on the "terminal" end of the bridge, it will flush the MAC address of
the now silent station (no problem here)
BUT
it will not forward packets from the "backbone" side until it hears
from the station on the far end again. 

That is correct, it waits for something from the "terminal-end" station.

One of our people spent a lot of time tracking this down, calling DEC,
having them say we must be wrong, and finally having them tell us that
this is THE WAY IT IS DESIGNED!  They pointed to an obscure document
which notes this.

DEC claims this a "workgroup" bridge and intended as a "leaf" bridge.

Apparently someone assumed all "workgroup" stations  generate some sort
of keep alive packets.  This is of course "less than adequate" for an
IP network.

Can someone please tell me this isn't so and we are just missing 
something extraordinarily simple?  Why would anyone design a bridge
like that?

