Message-ID: <2pjub0$q25@lime.rsmas.miami.edu>
NNTP-Posting-Host: lime.rsmas.miami.edu
Summary: f77 -O2 and above uses sincos primative and loses
Keywords: sincos

The following short FORTRAN program (if compiled -O or -O2/better)
demonstrates a SEVERE bug in the OSF/1 runtime library (libm) used by FORTRAN.
[Optimization levels O2 and above collapse occurrences of the SIN and
COS of the same argument into one RTL call to the routine "sincos(x)"
which returns both values.]

        program a

        real*8 x, y, z
        real*8 colat, lon, lat

        lat = -75d0
        colat = 90d0-lat

        do lon=-169d0,-172d0,-1d0
        x = dcosd(LON)*dsind(COLAT)
        y = dsind(LON)*dsind(COLAT)
        print *,sngl(lat),sngl(lon),sngl(x),sngl(y)
        end do

        end

Example of bad results (seen on both OSF/1 v1.3, rev 111 and
OSF/1 v2.0, rev 240 (and 'FT after v2.0', rev 248.1) using either
f77 t3.4 or f77 v3.4):

     LAT             LON          X              Y
  -75.00000      -169.0000     -0.2540638     -4.9385004E-02
  -75.00000      -170.0000     -0.2548870     -4.4943456E-02
  -75.00000      -171.0000      0.2556325     -4.0488217E-02
  -75.00000      -172.0000      0.2563002     -3.6020648E-02

Notice that X loses its '-' sign.   A similar result obtains for LON
between +169 and +172.

This bug has been reported to DEC (FT QARs 21221/21222).

Jim Brown, U Miami, jim@lime.rsmas.miami.edu
