In article <matuscak-120494114107@zaphod.rohrer.com>,
matuscak@rohrer.com (Joe Matuscak) writes:
|> In article <38433@dog.ee.lbl.gov>, ctday@randolf.lbl.gov (Christopher T.
|> Day) wrote:
|> 
|> > I have a new (arrived Friday) DECpcXL 560 with a Diamond Viper PCI
|> > graphics board. I have been unable to get the graphics board to reliably
|> > shift out of VGA mode under Windows 3.1. Has anyone else had this
|> > problem? Any suggestions?
|> 
|> I had a similar problem with a pair of XL 566s. I wasnt having it loose
|> sync, I just couldnt get it to do anything other than VGA in windows. (I
|> was using a Sony CPD106 and a NEC 6FG rather than a DEC one).  I *finally*
|> got it working after after about 3 weeks of beating up the local office and
|> getting a DEC PC engineering type on the phone.  Once I got the right
|> sequence of things to do, it worked fine.  There is some pretty flaky
|> things in the Viper install.
|> 
|> > I reinstalled Windows and the Viper drivers and at least got back to
|> > VGA. When I tried resolutions + colors that required more than 1Meg
|> 
|> A hint, you dont need to reinstall windows if the display driver gets
|> hosed. From dos, go to the windows directory and run setup. You can change
|> all of the Windows setup parameters from dos. (I found this out the hard
|> way too :-)
|> 
|> First thing you need to do is get the display set back to VGA. Delete all
|> of the viper stuff from the windows directory. Delete the Viper directory.
|> Reboot. Make sure you can get into Windows in VGA mode. Get back to dos and
|> install the Viper software from the floppy. In vprmode, use setup to
|> explicitly choose the monitor frequency and refresh rates.  Save those.
|> Tell it to update your autoexec. Reboot. Get into windows. Open the Viper
|> icon and select the resolution you want. Restart windows. Thats it.  Your
|> mileage may vary :-)
|> 
|> One problem that I recall hearing about, but not necessarily about these
|> systems is "smart" multisyunc monitors getting confused. The solution was
|> to lock the monitor into a specific mode rather than letting it try to
|> guess what the graphics card was set to.
|> 
|> Hope this helps.
|> 
|> 
|> -- 
