What I don't understand is how they intend to get past the restrictions
caused by the number of registers. The 21064 is supposed to have 
a floating point pipeline latency of 6
ie for full performance the result of an instruction should not be used until 
6 cycles after the instruction. 
This implies you ideally want to be interleaving the calculation of 6 
seperate expressions. 
The architecture specifies 32 registers of which r31 always returns zero
so this gives about 5 registers to each expression.
This may be ok for many purposes but it is a bit cramped for mine.
However if you increase the instruction issue without reducing the pipeline
latency then we will require a higher number of expressions to be calculated 
in parallel to obtain the full performance and the number of registers get
really tight.

There is obviously something I am misunderstanding here. Does anyone want to
enlighten me?

			spb


