I have heard that lots of people get 'illegal instruction' and
other strange errors with programs compiled on OSF/1 2.0. So do I !

In my case I'm 99% sure the problem comes from the new linker, since
the errors occur even when compiling the modules on OSF/1 1.3, and
doing only the final linking step on OSF/1 2.0.

In the debugger I have found that my executable code (compiled with gcc
if it matters) gets systematically corrupted above the address
0x120016000, thus explaining the strange errors reported. The next module
in the text segment, which is a library module (compiled with cc this time),
is intact.

Programs that are small enough not to reach this deadly address execute
perfectly well ...


If my assumption about the linker is correct, are there any new limits
imposed by the new version ?  Or maybe there is an address space collision
between code and data (the linker map does not report any clash).


Best regards,
Jarle.

Compiler lab, Swiss Federal Institute of Technology.



