In article <CnuIux.AM9@puma.uucp>, puma.uucp!john@becca.rain.com 
(John Meissen) writes:
|>
|>I recall an observation by someone that the behavior of floating 
|>point operations on the Alpha is "different" than other machines,
|>e.g. the MIPS boxes running Ultrix, but I can't recall what,
|>specifically, that behavior was. 
|>
|>I think there was something about avoiding conversions between
|>types, certain hardware support (or some lack thereof), and some
|>floating point results differing from what one might get on
|>a MIPS box.

I don't know of any difference of this nature.  If one sticks to the IEEE
floating format (OpenVMS AXP supports both IEEE and VAX floating), there
are no differences in how calculations or conversions are done.  Some
people have found differences in program results which have been traced to
different math library implementations (the one on DEC OSF/1 AXP is better.)

The only difference I can think of is if you have a Fortran application which
used the -fpe switch to specify non-default exception semantics.  Current
versions of DEC Fortran for DEC OSF/1 AXP don't support anything other than
the default -fpe 0, but this will change in a forthcoming release.  If
your application is in C, it's not an issue as DEC C already has the
necessary support.

If you can remember any more details of the supposed differences, please
send me mail and I'll help clarify the issue.
