Thank you for porting these.  

I tried them out Monday night, and I observed an interesting phenomenon.
The VAXC rand() function is -- well -- predictable.  In DEMINEUR.C, the
program generates row and column for mine placement pseudorandomly as
follows:

      l = 1 + ( int ) ( rand ( ) % nb_lig ) ;
      c = 1 + ( int ) ( rand ( ) % nb_col ) ;

nb_lig and nb_col default to 16 and 30 (note both are even).  I ran the game
a few times, and noticed all the mines were on every other row and every
other column.  This is because my VAXCRTL rand() *always* *alternates*
between even and odd values.  I tried dividing by 2, and the pattern became
even-even-odd-odd.  I finally divided by 32768, and it looks OK.

I RTFM-ed, and this gross weakness is not mentioned anywhere!  

I'm running VMS 6.0 with VAX C 3.2.  Is it me, or is it VAXC?  It's VAXC,
right?

