>> per second performance, bulk throughput has been dissapointing.  Something
>> around 3Mbytes/sec seems to swamp the i960 (HSJ40 cpu).  We have V1.1J
>> firmware and writing to 3 member RZ26 stripesets, striped by the HSJ.  The
>> data source is a V6620 with a CIXCD which should be able to go at 6M/s at
>> least.
> 
> Others have mentioned V1.3 and later firmware, so you might try updating.

There is no later.  V1.3J is the current production version.  We're a field
test site for 1.4 but it isn't shipping yet (although it's getting close).

> I have heard that the original release of the HSJ's had dual-pathing on
> the CI bus disabled as a workaround for some kind of race condition that was
> occurring, thus limiting throughput (or at least I/O's per second) significantly. 
> The current firmware release, together with the latest release of VMS (6.1),
> repairs this.

Incorrect.  Dual-pathing was enabled.  Under certain heavy load conditions, the
HSJ could crash/hang.  The workaround to this was to be manually disable the
second path (easy to do via software).  There is a problem with the workaround
in that you may experience stalls when accessing your disk drives.

We were able to easily duplicate the crash with the 1.3 firmware.  We have not
been able to crash the HSJ with the FT releases.  We're still running VMS
5.5-2.  VMS 6.1 has nothing to do with it (although V6.1 is supposed to
correctly identify the disk drive type for an RZ28).

> [By CI Bus dual-pathing, I mean simultaneous use of both paths.  The
> redundant path was still used if the first path failed.  Apparently this
> is the way HSC's work as well, or at least the HSC's that I have.]

HSCs can only transfer on one path at a time.  If the path fails, it falls over
to the alternate path.  HSJs can transfer on both paths at the same time from
different hosts.

Although the HSJ is still rated at less I/Os per second than an HSC, the HSJ
easily outperforms the HSC.  It's not even close!  This is just another case
where benchmarks can be very deceiving.  Part of the problem is that the HSC
can't achieve its advertised numbers in a production environment (for starters,
don't even think about turning caching on - it's 2ms alone for a cache lookup
therefore dropping your maximum theoretical top-end to under 500 IOs/sec)

