I have a new (arrived Friday) DECpcXL 560 with a Diamond Viper PCI
graphics board. I have been unable to get the graphics board to reliably
shift out of VGA mode under Windows 3.1. Has anyone else had this
problem? Any suggestions?

What I've tried so far:

MS-DOS 6.2 and Windows 3.1 came preinstalled. On the first boot of the
machine, Windows came up with VGA resolution. The first thing I tried to
do was change the display, using the Diamond Viper Control Panel Applet;
I picked 1024x780x65K since the graphics board has 2Meg and the monitor,
DEC's 15", is supposed to be able to handle it. I restarted Windows and
the monitor sync was completely lost - horizontal tearing and vertical rolling.

I reinstalled Windows and the Viper drivers and at least got back to
VGA. When I tried resolutions + colors that required more than 1Meg
video RAM, I got the tearing. If I tried 1024x780x256, the sync held,
but the resolution was still only VGA. The Control Panel Applet and
Windows Setup declared that I had, indeed, changed the driver and
resolution, but nothing more than VGA actually appeared.

Except for one case. In MS-DOS, I ran the VPRMODE application and
stepped through all the resolutions and centered them using the CUSTOM
monitor selection. I then started Windows, and, lo and behold, it came
up in 800x600x256, that last thing I had set. I tried other resolutions,
including 1024x780x65K, each time telling the Applet to Install New
drivers. (Since the Applet never actually called for the floppy, I
conclude that it copied the drivers from its \VIPER\WIN31 directory.)
All these resolutions worked. Ok, fine, I can believe in computer
superstitions as much as the next guy so long as they work, but a power
cycle lost it all - back to tearing at 1024x780x65K and only VGA
resolution on all other settings. Repeating the VPRMODE exercise didn't help.

I've reseated the Viper and tried another PCI slot to no avail. These
symptoms suggest to me that the software is correctly managing the
drivers, but that the Viper is not actually shifting modes when
instructed, i.e., Viper card is bad.

Any other ideas, analyses, suggestions? Thanks.


	- Chris


P.S. The VGA that I am getting does not seem to be centered according to
the what I had setup in VPRMODE; it is low and to the right. The
environment variables are set correctly in MS-DOS.


