In article <BKORT.94Apr5083256@copernicus.bbn.com>, bkort@copernicus.bbn.com (Barry Kort) writes...
>I have some RA81 drives that I would like to add to a MicroVAX II that
>already has an RA82 and and RA81 drive.  Do I have to set the Unit 
>Numbers on the new drives to match the number on the connector to the
>KDA-50 SDI Controller?

My UDA50 manual says to hook drive 0 to connector #3, drive 1 to connector
#2, drive 2 to connector #1, and drive 3 to connector #0.  I've violated
this rule many times, and the drives still works with the nonstandard
cabling.  So why did DEC recommend this reverse order?  I dunno.

> 
>If so, how do I set the Unit Numbers on an RA81?

Oh, the magic of "Unit plugs".  If the Ready light on your drive has
no number on it, then it is unit zero.  If it has a number on
it, that's the unit number.  If you pull out the Ready bulb
cover, you'll discover it has 8 prongs, and if you'll look closer,
you'll see they have "1-2-4-8-16-32-64-128" written next to them.
Unit number 0 has all 8 prongs present; unit 3 is missing prongs 1 and 2,
and so on.  I've never used drives with unit plugs higher than 3 on a
UDA50, but I've definitely had HSC50's with RA82's up to Unit number 7.
Someday I'll have to break off all the prongs and see if I can
really get unit number 255.

If you don't have the appropriate Unit Plugs you can either break the
prongs off an already existing unit plug, or buy them.  I think DEC
still sells them, but you can probably find a complete used RA80 for
less than DEC sells the appropriate unit plugs for.  (I'd be pleasantly
surprised if DEC wanted less than $20.00 for a new unit plug; they
have this nasty habit of marking up the prices of all obsolete equipment
by a fixed amount each year, with that fixed amount being much greater
than inflation.) 

