In article <BKORT.94Apr5083256@copernicus.bbn.com>, bkort@copernicus.bbn.com (Barry Kort) writes:
> I have some RA81 drives that I would like to add to a MicroVAX II that
> already has an RA82 and and RA81 drive.  Do I have to set the Unit 
> Numbers on the new drives to match the number on the connector to the
> KDA-50 SDI Controller?

  No. The SDI controllers assign no special numbering to their ports (but
some assign priority to the ports in some sort of sequence).

  Unit numbers on older SDI drives like the RA81/82 range from 0 to 250-
something (the highest numbers are reserved for "no plug inserted", "test",
etc.).

  Depending on your operating system, all drives may have to have unique
unit numbers (you can't have a Unit 0 on one controller and a Unit 0 on
another controller). In general, if the OS distinguishes between controllers
as part of device naming (for example, VMS uses DUA for the first controller,
DUB for the second controller, etc.) you can duplicate unit numbers as long
as the drives are on different controllers. This can lead to confusion,
though, so it's best to use unique numbers.

  Also, some drivers won't recognize unit numbers above a certain range. For
example, VMS [used to?] limit you to 4 MSCP controllers with 4 drives each
unless you were using a HSC. Thus, it would ignore drives with unit numbers
16 and up.

  In your particular case, I'd just use the next number(s) up from what you
are using now. Note that RA8x drives select units by breaking tabs off the
unit number plug, so you can't go back to a lower number after you've picked
a number.

