In article <i075c.n3.t1ad1fef3@email.teaser.com>, kilroy@email.teaser.com 
(philip peltier) writes:
|>I have an opportunity of purchasing at a really interesting price a DEC
|>computer, referenced on cpu as 'VAX II/GPX'. As I know few about dec
|>machines, could somebody tell me about the capabilities of this one?

This would be a VAXstation II/GPX.  The CPU is a MicroVAX II (.9 VUPS),
I/O bus is Q-BUS but it can have up to 14MB (I think) memory.  The GPX
refers to the graphics board which can handle 4 or 8 planes of color or
grayscale, depending on option installed.  Disk drives are the RD5x series,
ST506-style, not SCSI.  The disk controller is called RQDX3.  Many such
systems also had a TK50 tape drive (with its TQK50 controller). 

There are two configurations, the narrower pedestal BA23 box has limited
expansion space, the larger BA123 box on casters is more expandable.

I used one daily for several years - it's reliable but not particularly
fast compared to modern machines.  You'd want to make sure it has a big
enough disk to support the operating system you choose to run on it.
