As far as I'm concerned, SGI can keep the MIPS architecture :-).

You cannot run a single MIPS Ultrix program on a SGI MIPS machine
(wrong byte order and wrong operating system). You can run almost
all MIPS Ultrix programs on Alpha DEC OSF/1 by virtue of the "mx"
(aka DECmigrate) binary translator. And the translated executables
will run faster on a 200 Mhz Alpha than a 150 Mhz MIPS R4400 can run
them when they are compiled native for it:

		    		   SPECint92		SPECfp92

DEC 3000/800, native:  		   130.2 		184.0		(Oct93)
DEC 3000/800, translated:	   111.0 		120.0	        (Apr94)
SGI Challenge 150MHz/R4400/4MB:	    94.0		105.0		(May93)

The numbers for the SGI machine may not be current, so correct
