In article <1994Mar31.124835.21641@oxvax>, atmos@vax.oxford.ac.uk writes:
> 
> A quick question:
> 
> I am shortly going to add my own DEC Alpha 3000/300X workstation to an existing 
> VMS cluster, the system manager assures me it is impossible to set up the
> machine so that only I can log into it, i.e. only allow certain users to
> login, is this true ? 

        Your system manager is lying  to  you. :-)   Well, if s/he's not
    lying, then s/he needs some more training...

        Perhaps the simplest way to accomplish what you want is to put a
    check in SYS$SYLOGIN (which  is  usually  defined to be SYS$MANAGER:
    SYLOGIN.COM):  if F$GETSYI("SCSNODE") returns the Alpha's node name,
    and if F$GETJPI(0,"USERNAME")  returns  something  other  than  your
    username,  issue a message and LOGOUT.  You'll want to be a _little_
    more sophisticated than that  and  NOT  logout  users  in any of the
    SYSTEM groups, etc.

        Reliability of this depends  on  having  SYS$SYLOGIN defined (in
    the  SYSTEM  table)  so  that /NOCOMMAND  at  login  CANNOT  prevent
    SYLOGIN.COM from being run.
 
> I can set suitable priorities on the batch queues, but I was told anybody 
> could gain interactive use of the workstation ! Would it be easier to set 
> up access security under OSF/1 ? (I have the choice of clusters to join).

        I think I've demonstrated  above  that OpenVMS _easily_ supports
    the  access  restrictions you desire.  I provided one method,  there
    are many other possibilities (here's one:  GRANT  an  identifier  to
    those  people for whom you would _like_ to make the Alpha available,
    and check for that identifier  in  SYLOGIN).  I have no knowledge of
    OSF/1's capabilities in this respect...
 
        -Ken
