In article <CnpLp9.5zn@acsu.buffalo.edu>, kalisiak@cs.buffalo.edu (Chris Kalisiak) writes:
> Silly me. I thought that adding a new device was automatic, like under
> VMS. I should have known better -- this is unix, and you have to modify
> the kernal specification file, rebuild it, then reboot...
> 
> Why can't unix have a SYSGEN AUTOCONFIG ALL? 

  Most Unixes (I can't speak for Ultrix, I've never used it) let you configure
all of the possible devices that might be present (for example, on a KDA50
that's 4 "ra" devices), whether or not they exist on any given system. As long
as you have the device special files (/dev/ra*), they should happily auto-
configure and work, simply by plugging the drive in and booting.

  In fact, this is how the "generic" kernel works. However, in order to keep
the size of the generic kernel down, it usually doesn't include *all* of the
possible units for each controller.

  The Unix boxes I run here have SCSI buses, and I configure their kernels for
7 disks and 7 tapes per SCSI bus, and it figures out how many of each I really
have each time I boot.

  Some people with a "small system" mindset only configure the actual hardware
on the box when building a kernel (some consultants also do this to generate
more business when devices are added). This saves a small amount of memory (I
suspect the difference between 1 RA90 and 4 RA90's is easily under 4Kb).

