In article <1994Mar31.065807.529@rlgsc.com>, gezelter@rlgsc.com writes:
> In article <1994Mar29.104943.8134@siemens.co.at>, chris@mx2306.gud.siemens.co.at (Chris Mutschlechner) writes:
>> Where can I get a specification of the decnet protocol (ftp.sites, ...)?
>  
> Contact your Digital office. Most of the protocol specifications 
> are published by Digital (with the note in the front of each 
> specification document that no license fees are required to 
> legally implement the protocol).

Actually they don't mention implementation or license fees.  They do state that
the documents themselves may be copied, in whole or in part, as long as the
copyright notice is preserved and as long as the copy states that it's derived
from the DEC specification.  

Most of us have interpreted this to mean that derivative works (including
protocol implementations) are ok too, though technically, the right to make a
derivative work is separate from the right to make copies, and giving away one
doesn't give away the other.  In practice, there have been many third-party
implementations of DNA Phase IV, and Digital is fully aware of these and has
taken no action against their authors or distributors, so we're on safe ground.

> I would offer the part numbers, but the binder with my set of 
> protocol definitions is not near my desk at the moment.

Here they are.  The doc titles and order numbers are from a list posted years
ago by Joel Snyder; the descriptions are mine.  I invite corrections and
additional info.  

Phase IV General Description AA-N149A-TC

	An overview, shows what the various protocols do in the overall
	DNA.  REcommended. 

4.0 DDCMP AA-D588A-TC	obsolete
4.1 DDCMP AA-K176A-TK

	DDCMP is DEC's old vendor-specific (not proprietary, damn it!) 
	data link protocol for point-to-point and multidrop links.  These
	docs are probably of historical interest only. 

2.0 Ethernet (the blue book) AA-K759B-TK
1.0 Ethernet Data Link Func Spec AA-Y298A-TK
1.0 Ethernet Product Arch Spec AA-X440A-TK

	Useful if you are writing a new DECnet data link driver that is to
	replace or act like existing Ethernet drivers.  

2.0 Routing Layer Func Spec AA-X435A-TK

	Useful if you're building either a router or an end node (which covers
	just about everything :-) - end nodes contain a vestigial routing 
	layer, and its functions are described in this along with the functions
	of a routing node. 

3.2 NSP Func Spec AA-K176A-TK	obsolete
4.0 NSP Func Spec AA-X439A-TK
1.0 Session Control Func Spec AA-K182A-TK

	The above describe how to implement logical links -- what some
	protocol stacks call virtual circuits.  Necessary if you're building
	an end node.  Not strictly necessary if you're creating a standalone
	router, but it really is useful to know what happens at the next layer
	up, so I recommend these anyway.  Note that the "session control" layer
	is really very tightly associated with the NSP or network layer, and 
	was only broken out separate in DNA Phase IV because OSI did it that 
	way ("now we have seven layers just like OSI").  

5.6 DAP Func Spec AA-K177A-TK
2.0 NVT Foundation Services AA-DY89A-TK
2.0 NVT CTERM AA-DY88A-TK
4.0 Net Mgmt Func Spec AA-X437A-TK

	These describe three of the DEC-defined application layer protocols.
	DAP is used for DECnet remote file access.  NVT = Network Virtual 
	Terminal, ie CTERM is one of the ways that SET HOST works.  
	The Network Management Functional Spec is useful if you want to
	write code that does some of what NCP does (allow you to look at and
	change 	DECnet parameters from inside a program).  

3.0 MOP Func Spec AA-X436A-TK

	MOP is maintenance operations protocol, a bag sort of hung on the side
	of the rest of the protocol stack.  It's sort of an application-layer
	protocol that talks directly to the data link layer; it isn't routed. 
	Used for remote console access to "black boxes" like terminal servers
	and routers, and for downline loading and upline dumping of same, and
	for the initial phase (not all) of downline loading of Ethernet-booted 
	VAXstations.  

	--- Jamie Hanrahan, Kernel Mode Systems, San Diego CA
