>
>     I am using a VAX workstation and don't have direct access to Internet.  I
> tried to FTP a file thru email and since the file is quite large I received
> it in several mail messages.  Would you please help me with the following
> questions:
>
> 1/  Is there a program out there that can concatenate all parts together?  If
>     there is one then where can I find it?
>
> 2/  Since the file was UUENCODE it before it was mailed, is there an UUDECODE
>     program out there and where can I find it?
>
> 3/  Where can I find an UNZIP program for VAX/VMS?

        In sort of  reverse  order,  UUCODE  (includes both UUDECODE and
    UUENCODE),  ZIP  and UNZIP for VMS systems in available from  Hunter
    Goatley's file server (i.e.  via  e-mail),  FILESERV@WKUVX1.WKU.EDU.
    Additionally,  you  can  get  the  program PACKASM which will nicely
    extract  and  concatenate   (and   optionally  UUDECODE)  multi-part
    messages from MAIL or from NEWS.  More info below...

        However, to even get started with  the stuff from WKUVX1, you'll
    need  to go through a by-hand extraction the first time.  I  haven't
    used the e-mail FTP services, however the  file  servers  (for  VMS)
    essentailly  all use VMS_SHARE format for distribution.  You need to
    extract  these  from  MAIL  into  separate  disk  files  (use  MAIL>
    EXTRACT),  and then use DCL's APPEND command to assemble them into a
    single file.  Once assembled, you execute the file as a DCL  command
    file (that is, you do $@file) which actually extracts the files that
    have been transferred.

        To retrieve  the  above  packages,  send  a  mail  message which
    contains the following lines to FILESERV@WKUVX1.WKU.EDU:

    SEND PACKASM            !Comes as  5 60-block files                 
    SEND UUCODE             !Comes as  5 60-block files                 
    SEND FILESERV_TOOLS     !Needed if you don't have MFTU and UNZIP    
                                                                        
    Including the command DIR ALL on a separate line will return a brief
    listing of all the packages available from FILESERV@WKUVX1.WKU.EDU. 

        -Ken
